rm setting, reflecting real-life agricultural practices of the 1950s."},{": "charlottes-web", "context": "The cultural impact of "Charlotte's Web" has led to its inclusion in educational curricula, shaping children's understanding of compassion."}], "places": [{"name": "Brooklyn, USA", "role": "Birthplace of E.B. White"}, {"name": "Maine, USA", "role": "Inspiration for Setting"}, {"name": "New York, USA", "role": "Publication Site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Charlotte's Web", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "charlottes-web"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q45735", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotte%27s_Web"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Charlotte's Web left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 83 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": ""Charlotte's Web" significantly impacted children's literature and animal welfare discussions, selling over 45 million copies worldwide and being integrated into educational curricula.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-08T01:36:51Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27111092777", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1952 children's novel by e. b. white Created by E. White.", "newValue": ""Charlotte's Web," published in 1952 by E.B. White, is a pivotal children's novel that has transcended its genre to become a cultural touchstone in discussions about friendship, life, and mortality. Set predominantly in rural America, the narrative unfolds on a farm where the bond between a pig named Wilbur and a spider named Charlotte blossoms into an enduring tale of loyalty and sacrifice. White's masterful storytelling not only captivated young readers but also ignited conversations about the emotional complexities of animals and the importance of compassion in human experiences.

The story begins when Wilbur, a runt pig, is saved from slaughter by a young girl named Fern Arable. As Wilbur grows, he faces the grim reality of becoming a farm commodity, which prompts Charlotte to spin a web that proclaims him as "Some Pig," thus elevating his status and ensuring his survival. Through cleverly crafted words and a series of events, the novel showcases themes of friendship, loyalty, and the cycle of life, culminating in Charlotte's selfless act of sacrifice to save Wilbur. The book has sold over 45 million copies worldwide, establishing itself as a staple in children's literature and educational curricula.

The legacy of "Charlotte's Web" is profound, having inspired adaptations in film, theater, and even ballet, thus embedding it firmly in the cultural fabric of society. Its themes resonate across generations, teaching values of empathy and stewardship towards all living beings. The novel has been instrumental in sparking discussions about animal rights and welfare, influencing how children and adults alike view their interpersonal relationships with animals. As E.B. White once remarked, 'I think of the day I spent with Charlotte and Wilbur as a day well spent.'"}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-08T01:36:51Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27111092777", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Cultural, artistic, or intellectual creation"], "newValue": ["The rise of children's literature as a distinct genre in the early 20th century provided a fertile ground for E.B. White's creative expression.", "Post-World War II America saw an increased focus on family values and childhood innocence, making the themes of the novel particularly resonant.", "E.B. White's own experiences in rural settings influenced his portrayal of farm life and animal characters, reflecting a deep connection to nature."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-08T01:36:51Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27111092777", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": [""Charlotte's Web" has sold over 45 million copies, making it one of the best-selling children's books of all time.", "The novel has inspired multiple adaptations in film and theater, which have further entrenched its themes in popular culture.", "It has prompted discussions and educational initiatives around animal welfare and empathy in children, influencing how literature is used in moral education."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-08T01:36:51Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27111092777", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "charlottes-web", "s